bottom 40% of the population by impact - Agriculture Revenues (update)','','',87,3),(446,'CC.CHIC.CFPI.AI','Change in income (%) for bottom 40% of the population by impact - All impacts (update)','','',87,3),(447,'CC.CHIC.CFPI.DI','Change in income (%) for bottom 40% of the population by impact - Disasters (update)','','',87,3),(448,'CC.CHIC.CFPI.FP','Change in income (%) for bottom 40% of the population by impact - Food prices (update)','','',87,3),(449,'CC.CHIC.CFPI.HE','Change in income (%) for bottom 40% of the population by impact - Health (update)','','',87,3),(450,'CC.CHIC.CFPI.LP','Change in income (%) for bottom 40% of the population by impact - Labor productivity (update)','','',87,3),(451,'CC.COAL.PROD.OP','Annual coal production (Mt per year) - Operating','','',87,3),(452,'CC.COAL.PROD.PR','Annual coal production (Mt per year) - Proposed Projects (Total)','','',87,3),(453,'CC.ECA.GAEX.CA','Capacity of Liquefied Natural Gas export terminals (Mt per year) - Cancelled','','',87,3),(454,'CC.ECA.GAEX.ID','Capacity of Liquefied Natural Gas export terminals (Mt per year) - In Development (Proposed + Construction)','','',87,3),(455,'CC.ECA.GAEX.OP','Capacity of Liquefied Natural Gas export terminals (Mt per year) - Operating','','',87,3),(456,'CC.ECA.GAEX.SH','Capacity of Liquefied Natural Gas export terminals (Mt per year) - Shelved','','',87,3),(457,'CC.ECA.GAIM.CA','Capacity of Liquefied Natural Gas import terminals (Mt per year) - Cancelled','','',87,3),(458,'CC.ECA.GAIM.ID','Capacity of Liquefied Natural Gas import terminals (Mt per year) - In Development (Proposed + Construction)','','',87,3),(459,'CC.ECA.GAIM.OP','Capacity of Liquefied Natural Gas import terminals (Mt per year) - Operating','','',87,3),(460,'CC.ECA.GAIM.SH','Capacity of Liquefied Natural Gas import terminals (Mt per year) - Shelved','','',87,3),(461,'CC.EG.CONS.COAL.PC','Per capita daily coal consumption (short tons per capita per day)','Per capita data has been generated by World Bank staff by taking Energy consumption data [U.S EIA] and applying country population data from the World Bank Group - World Development Indicators.','',87,3),(462,'CC.EG.CONS.GAS.PC','Per capita daily gas consumption (thousand cubic feet per capita per day)','Per capita data has been generated by World Bank staff by taking Energy consumption data [U.S EIA] and applying country population data from the World Bank Group - World Development Indicators.','',87,3),(463,'CC.EG.CONS.OIL.PC','Per capita daily oil consumption (barrels per capita per day)','Per capita data has been generated by World Bank staff by taking Energy consumption data [U.S EIA] and applying country population data from the World Bank Group - World Development Indicators.','',87,3),(464,'CC.EG.SUBF.PC','Fossil-fuel pre-tax subsidies (consumption and production) USD per capita','This indicator presents the pre-tax subsidies (consumption and production) on fossil fuels as an index of USD per capita. Data is taken from the United Nations Sustainable Goals, representing Indicator 12.c.1: Fossil-fuel subsidies (consumption and production) per capita (constant United States dollars).','',87,3),(465,'CC.ELEC.CON','Electricity net consumption','','',87,3),(466,'CC.ENTX.ENE.ZS','Total energy tax revenue (% of GDP)','Revenues from taxes raised on energy products (fossil fuels and electricity) including those used in transportation (petrol and diesel). This includes all CO2-related taxes.','',87,3),(467,'CC.ENTX.ENV.ZS','Total environmental tax revenue (% of GDP)','','',87,3),(468,'CC.ENV.TRAD.EX','Trade in environmental goods as share of total exports (%)','Environmental goods include both goods connected to environmental protection—such as goods related to pollution management and resource management—and adapted goods—which are goods that have been specifically modified to be more “environmentally friendly” or “cleaner.”    A relatively high share of environmental goods imports indicates that a country purchases a significant share of environmental goods from other countries. A relatively high share of environmental goods exports indicates that a country produces and sells a significant share of environmental goods to other countries. A country’s environmental goods trade balance is the difference between its exports and imports of environmental goods.','',87,3),(469,'CC.ENV.TRAD.IM','Trade in environmental goods as share of total imports (%)','Environmental goods include both goods connected to environmental protection—such as goods related to pollution management and resource management—and adapted goods—which are goods that have been specifically modified to be more “environmentally friendly” or “cleaner.”    A relatively high share of environmental goods imports indicates that a country purchases a significant share of environmental goods from other countries. A relatively high share of environmental goods exports indicates that a country produces and sells a significant share of environmental goods to other countries. A country’s environmental goods trade balance is the difference between its exports and imports of environmental goods.','',87,3),(470,'CC.ESG.AGFE','Employment by sector and gender (% of total) - Agriculture - Female','Employment of Females within Agriculture is disaggregated from the Global Jobs Indicators Database. The Database covers socio-demographics, labor force status and employment type, employment composition by sector and occupation, education level completed, hours worked, and earnings. The database was compiled from national surveys and subnational microdata which was first harmonized for the Bank-wide I2D2 database, then quality checked by the Jobs Group.','',87,3),(471,'CC.ESG.AGMA','Employment by sector and gender (% of total) - Agriculture - Male','Employment of Males within Agriculture is disaggregated from the Global Jobs Indicators Database. The Database covers socio-demographics, labor force status and employment type, employment composition by sector and occupation, education level completed, hours worked, and earnings. The database was compiled from national surveys and subnational microdata which was first harmonized for the Bank-wide I2D2 database, then quality checked by the Jobs Group.','',87,3),(472,'CC.ESG.CMFE','Employment by sector and gender (% of total) - Commerce - Female','Employment of Females within Commerce is disaggregated from the Global Jobs Indicators Database. The Database covers socio-demographics, labor force status and employment type, employment composition by sector and occupation, education level completed, hours worked, and earnings. The database was compiled from national surveys and subnational microdata which was first harmonized for the Bank-wide I2D2 database, then quality checked by the Jobs Group.','',87,3),(473,'CC.ESG.CMMA','Employment by sector and gender (% of total) - Commerce - Male','Employment of Males within Commerce is disaggregated from the Global Jobs Indicators Database. The Database covers socio-demographics, labor force status and employment type, employment composition by sector and occupation, education level completed, hours worked, and earnings. The database was compiled from national surveys and subnational microdata which was first harmonized for the Bank-wide I2D2 database, then quality checked by the Jobs Group.','',87,3),(474,'CC.ESG.CNFE','Employment by sector and gender (% of total) - Construction - Female','Employment of Females within Construction is disaggregated from the Global Jobs Indicators Database. The Database covers socio-demographics, labor force status and employment type, employment composition by sector and occupation, education level completed, hours worked, and earnings. The database was compiled from national surveys and subnational microdata which was first harmonized for the Bank-wide I2D2 database, then quality checked by the Jobs Group.','',87,3),(475,'CC.ESG.CNMA','Employment by sector and gender (% of total) - C