stration is disaggregated from the Global Jobs Indicators Database. The Database covers socio-demographics, labor force status and employment type, employment composition by sector and occupation, education level completed, hours worked, and earnings. The database was compiled from national surveys and subnational microdata which was first harmonized for the Bank-wide I2D2 database, then quality checked by the Jobs Group.','',87,3),(490,'CC.ESG.PSFE','Employment by sector and gender (% of total) - Public sector employment - Female','Employment of Females within Public sector employment is disaggregated from the Global Jobs Indicators Database. The Database covers socio-demographics, labor force status and employment type, employment composition by sector and occupation, education level completed, hours worked, and earnings. The database was compiled from national surveys and subnational microdata which was first harmonized for the Bank-wide I2D2 database, then quality checked by the Jobs Group.','',87,3),(491,'CC.ESG.PSMA','Employment by sector and gender (% of total) - Public sector employment - Male','Employment of Males within Public sector employment is disaggregated from the Global Jobs Indicators Database. The Database covers socio-demographics, labor force status and employment type, employment composition by sector and occupation, education level completed, hours worked, and earnings. The database was compiled from national surveys and subnational microdata which was first harmonized for the Bank-wide I2D2 database, then quality checked by the Jobs Group.','',87,3),(492,'CC.ESG.SEFE','Employment by sector and gender (% of total) - Services - Female','Employment of Females within Services is disaggregated from the Global Jobs Indicators Database. The Database covers socio-demographics, labor force status and employment type, employment composition by sector and occupation, education level completed, hours worked, and earnings. The database was compiled from national surveys and subnational microdata which was first harmonized for the Bank-wide I2D2 database, then quality checked by the Jobs Group.','',87,3),(493,'CC.ESG.SEMA','Employment by sector and gender (% of total) - Services - Male','Employment of Males within Services is disaggregated from the Global Jobs Indicators Database. The Database covers socio-demographics, labor force status and employment type, employment composition by sector and occupation, education level completed, hours worked, and earnings. The database was compiled from national surveys and subnational microdata which was first harmonized for the Bank-wide I2D2 database, then quality checked by the Jobs Group.','',87,3),(494,'CC.ESG.TCFE','Employment by sector and gender (% of total) - Transport & Communication - Female','Employment of Females within Transport & Communication is disaggregated from the Global Jobs Indicators Database. The Database covers socio-demographics, labor force status and employment type, employment composition by sector and occupation, education level completed, hours worked, and earnings. The database was compiled from national surveys and subnational microdata which was first harmonized for the Bank-wide I2D2 database, then quality checked by the Jobs Group.','',87,3),(495,'CC.ESG.TCMA','Employment by sector and gender (% of total) - Transport & Communication - Male','Employment of Males within Transport & Communication is disaggregated from the Global Jobs Indicators Database. The Database covers socio-demographics, labor force status and employment type, employment composition by sector and occupation, education level completed, hours worked, and earnings. The database was compiled from national surveys and subnational microdata which was first harmonized for the Bank-wide I2D2 database, then quality checked by the Jobs Group.','',87,3),(496,'CC.FGHG.SYS.CS','Food GHG emissions by system stage (Share of total) - Consumption','Food GHG emissions disaggregated by system stage are generated from EDGAR-FOOD, a global emission inventory of GHGs from the food systems. EDGAR-FOOD has been developed to aid the understanding of the activities underlying the energy demand and use, agriculture and land use change emissions associated with the production, distribution, consumption and disposal of food through the various stages and sectors of the composite global food system. These data were complemented with data from the FAOSTAT database on GHG emissions from land use related to agriculture (FAO, 2020). EDGAR-FOOD represents the first database consistently covering each stage of the food chain for all countries with yearly frequency for the period 1990-2015. Details regarding the methodology applied are available in Crippa et al. (2021).','',87,3),(497,'CC.FGHG.SYS.LU','Food GHG emissions by system stage (Share of total) - LULUC (Production)','Food GHG emissions disaggregated by system stage are generated from EDGAR-FOOD, a global emission inventory of GHGs from the food systems. EDGAR-FOOD has been developed to aid the understanding of the activities underlying the energy demand and use, agriculture and land use change emissions associated with the production, distribution, consumption and disposal of food through the various stages and sectors of the composite global food system. These data were complemented with data from the FAOSTAT database on GHG emissions from land use related to agriculture (FAO, 2020). EDGAR-FOOD represents the first database consistently covering each stage of the food chain for all countries with yearly frequency for the period 1990-2015. Details regarding the methodology applied are available in Crippa et al. (2021).','',87,3),(498,'CC.FGHG.SYS.PD','Food GHG emissions by system stage (Share of total) - Production','Food GHG emissions disaggregated by system stage are generated from EDGAR-FOOD, a global emission inventory of GHGs from the food systems. EDGAR-FOOD has been developed to aid the understanding of the activities underlying the energy demand and use, agriculture and land use change emissions associated with the production, distribution, consumption and disposal of food through the various stages and sectors of the composite global food system. These data were complemented with data from the FAOSTAT database on GHG emissions from land use related to agriculture (FAO, 2020). EDGAR-FOOD represents the first database consistently covering each stage of the food chain for all countries with yearly frequency for the period 1990-2015. Details regarding the methodology applied are available in Crippa et al. (2021).','',87,3),(499,'CC.GHG.MEMG.EI','Macro drivers of GHG emissions growth in the period 2012-2018 - Emission Intensity of GDP','Data reflects the impact of the emissions intensity of GDP (as a macro-driver) to total emission growth (excluding LUCF) across the period 2012-2018. This data has been calculated by World Bank staff using greenhouse gas emissions data from the World Resource Institute\'s Climate Watch. Climate Watch Historical Emission data contains sector-level greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions data for 194 countries and the European Union (EU) for the period 1990-2018, including emissions of the six major GHGs from most major sources and sinks. Non-CO2 emissions are expressed in CO2 equivalents using 100-year global warming potential values from IPCC Fourth Assessment Report. Climate Watch Historical GHG Emissions data (previously published through CAIT Climate Data Explorer) are derived from several sources. Any use of the Land-Use Change and Forestry or Agriculture indicator should be cited as FAO 2020, FAOSTAT Emissions Database. Any use of CO2 emissions from fuel combustion data should be cited as CO2 Emissions from Fuel Combustion, OECD/IEA, 2020.','',87,3),(500,'CC.GHG.MEMG.GC','Macro drivers of GHG emissions growth in the period 2012-2018 - GDP per capita','Data reflects the impact of GDP-per capita (as a macro-driver) to total emission growth (excluding LUCF) across the period 2012-2018. This data has been calculated by World Bank staff using greenhouse gas emissions data from the World Resource Institute\'s Climate Watch. Climate Watch Historical Emission data contains sector-level greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions data for 194 countries and the European Union (EU) for the period 1990-2018, including emissions of the six major GHGs from most major sources and sinks. Non-CO2 emissions are expressed in CO2 equivalents using 100-year global warming potential values from IPCC Fourth Assessment Report. Climate Watch Historical GHG Emissions data (previously published through CAIT Climate Data Explorer) are derived from several sources. Any use of the Land-Use Change and Forestry or Agriculture indicator should be cited as FAO 2020, FAOSTAT Emissions Database. Any use of CO2 emissions from fuel combustion data should be cited as CO2 Emissions from Fuel Combustion, OECD/IEA, 2020.','',87,3),(501,'CC.INCP.ALRS','Annual investment needs for coastal protection, by risk strategy (% of GDP) - low risk tolerance','Data reflects the cost of a risk taking strategy that adopts an ambititious low-risk strategy. This is discussed in Policy Note 5 of the Beyond the Gap report.','',87,3),(502,'CC.INCP.KRGC','Annual investment needs for coastal protection, by risk strategy (% of GDP) - constant relative flood risk','Data reflects the cost of a risk taking strategy that keeps the risk relative to GDP constant. This is discussed in Policy Note 5 of the Beyond the Gap report.','',87,3),(503,'CC.INCP.SPMC','Annual investment needs for coastal protection, by risk strategy (% of GDP) - optimal protection','Data reflects the level of spending in protection that minimizes the cost of floods, including the prevention of expenditure and damages. This is discussed in Policy Note 5 of the Beyond the Gap report.','',87,3),(504,'CC.ISG.FFFE','Informal employment by sector and gender (% of total) - Agriculture, forestry and fishing - Female','This indicator presents the share of agriculture (female) employment which is classified as informal employment in the total economy. Data is taken from the United Nations Sustainable Goals, representing Indicator 8.3.1: Proportion of informal employment in total employment, by sector and sex.','',87,3),(505,'CC.ISG.FFMA','Informal employment by sector and gender (% of total) - Agriculture, forestry and fishing - Male','This indicator presents the share of agriculture (male) employment which is classified as informal employment in the total economy. Data is taken from the United Nations Sustainable Goals, representing Indicator 8.3.1: Proportion of informal employment in total employment, by sector and sex.','',87,3),(506,'CC.ISG.NAFE','Informal employment by sector and gender (% of total) - Non-agriculture - Female','This indicator presents the share of non-agriculture (female) employment which is classified as informal employment in the total economy. Data is taken from the United Nations Sustainable Goals, representing Indicator 8.3.1: Proportion of informal employment in total employment, by sector and sex.','',87,3),(507,'CC.ISG.NAMA','Informal employment by sector and gender (% of total) - Non-agriculture - Male','This indicator presents the share of non-agriculture (male) employment which is classified as informal employment in the total economy. Data is taken from the United Nations Sustainable Goals, representing Indicator 8.3.1: Proportion of informal employment in total employment, by sector and sex.','',87,3),(508,'CC.ISG.NBFE','Informal employment by sector and gender (% of total) - No breakdown - Female','This indicator presents the share of employment that had no-breakdown as either non-agricultural or agricultural (female), which is classified as informal employment in the total economy. Data is taken from the United Nations Sustainable Goals, representing Indicator 8.3.1: Proportion of informal employment in total employment, by sector and sex.','',87,3),(509,'CC.ISG.NBMA','Informal employment by sector and gender (% of total) - No breakdown - Male','This indicator presents the share of employment that had no-breakdown as either non-agricultural or agricultural (male), which is classified as informal employment in the total economy. Data is taken from the United Nations Sustainable Goals, representing Indicator 8.3.1: Proportion of informal employment in total employment, by sector and sex.','',87,3),(510,'CC.NEFO.IMCR.BA','Net food imports by crop - Barley','Net food imports are generated from FAOSTAT, taking Export level (tonnes) from the Import level (tonnes) [both under Trade: Crops and Livestock Products]. Net food imports are a proxy for the country\'s vulnerability to global food prices.','',87,3),(511,'CC.NEFO.IMCR.BP','Net food imports by crop - Rice, paddy','Net food imports are generated from FAOSTAT, taking Export level (tonnes) from the Import level (tonnes) [both under Trade: Crops and Livestock Products]. Net food imports are a proxy for the country\'s vulnerability to global food prices.','',87,3),(512,'CC.NEFO.IMCR.MA','Net food imports by crop - Maize','Net food imports are generated from FAOSTAT, taking Export level (tonnes) from the Import level (tonnes) [both under Trade: Crops and Livestock Products]. Net food imports are a proxy for the country\'s vulnerability to global food prices.','',87,3),(513,'CC.NEFO.IMCR.RB','Net food imports by crop - Rice, broken','Net food imports are generated from FAOSTAT, taking Export level (tonnes) from the Import level (tonnes) [both under Trade: Crops and Livestock Products]. Net food imports are a proxy for the country\'s vulnerability to global food prices.','',87,3),(514,'CC.NEFO.IMCR.RE','Net food imports by crop - Rice, paddy (rice milled equivalent)','Net food imports are generated from FAOSTAT, taking Export level (tonnes) from the Import level (tonnes) [both under Trade: Crops and Livestock Products]. Net food imports are a proxy for the country\'s vulnerability to global food prices.','',87,3),(515,'CC.NEFO.IMCR.RH','Net food imports by crop - Rice, husked','Net food imports are generated from FAOSTAT, taking Export level (tonnes) from the Import level (tonnes) [both under Trade: Crops and Livestock Products]. Net food imports are a proxy for the country\'s vulnerability to global food prices.','',87,3),(516,'CC.NEFO.IMCR.RI','Net food imports by crop - Rice, milled/husked','Net food imports are generated from FAOSTAT, taking Export level (tonnes) from the Import level (tonnes) [both under Trade: Crops and Livestock Products]. Net food imports are a proxy for the country\'s vulnerability to global food prices.','',87,3),(517,'CC.NEFO.IMCR.RM','Net food imports by crop - Rice, milled','Net food imports are generated from FAOSTAT, taking Export level (tonnes) from the Import level (tonnes) [both under Trade: Crops and Livestock Products]. Net food imports are a proxy for the country\'s vulnerability to global food prices.','',87,3),(518,'CC.NEFO.IMCR.SB','Net food imports by crop - Soybeans','Net food imports are generated from FAOSTAT, taking Export level (tonnes) from the Import level (tonnes) [both under Trade: Crops and Livestock Products]. Net food imports are a proxy for the country\'s vulnerability to global food prices.','',87,3),(519,'CC.NEFO.IMCR.WH','Net food imports by crop - Wheat','Net food imports are generated from FAOSTAT, taking Export level (tonnes) from the Import level (tonnes) [both under Trade: Crops and Livestock Products]. Net food imports are a proxy for the country\'s vulnerability to global food prices.','',87,3),(520,'CC.RISK.AST.ZS','Risk to asset (average annual losses as % of GDP)','Risk to asset indicator is intended to be viewed with risk to wellbeing indicator (both reported as average annual losses as % of GDP). The Unbreakable report which the indicators are taken from moves beyond asset and production losses and shifts its attention to how natural disasters affect people’s well-being. Disasters are far greater threats to well-being than traditional estimates suggest. This approach provides a more nuanced view of natural disasters than usual reporting, and a perspective that takes fuller account of poor people’s vulnerabilities.','',87,3),(521,'CC.RISK.WELL.ZS','Risk to wellbeing (average annual losses as % of GDP)','Risk to wellbeing indicator is intended to be viewed with risk to asset indicator (both reported as average annual losses as % of GDP). The Unbreakable report which the indicators are taken from moves beyond asset and production losses and shifts its attention to how natural disasters affect people’s well-being. Disasters are far greater threats to well-being than traditional estimates suggest. This approach provides a more nuanced view of natural disasters than usual reporting, and a perspective that takes fuller account of poor people’s vulnerabilities.','',87,3),(522,'CC.SP.EXP.ZS','Public social protection expenditure (%of GDP)','','',87,3),(523,'CC.TNET.INV.ZS','Annual investments needed to make transport infrastructure more resilient by 2030 (% of baseline investment costs)','','',87,3),(524,'cfuel_any_dfcl_cogn_all','Persons with clean cooking fuel (% of persons with any degree of cognitive difficulty)','','',92,3),(525,'CM.FIN.INTL.GD.ZS','Financing via international capital markets (gross inflows, % of GDP)','','',57,3),(526,'CM.MKT.LCAP.CD','Market capitalization of listed domestic companies (current US$)','Market capitalization (also known as market value) is the share price times the number of shares outstanding (including their several classes) for listed domestic companies. Investment funds, unit trusts, and companies whose only business goal is to hold shares of other listed companies are excluded. Data are end of year values converted to U.S. dollars using corresponding year-end foreign exchange rates.','',2,7),(527,'CM.MKT.LCAP.GD.ZS','Market capitalization of listed domestic companies (% of GDP)','Market capitalization (also known as market value) is the share price times the number of shares outstanding (including their several classes) for listed domestic companies. Investment funds, unit trusts, and companies whose only business goal is to hold shares of other listed companies are excluded. Data are end of year values.','',2,7),(528,'CM.MKT.TRAD.CD','Stocks traded, total value (current US$)','The value of shares traded is the total number of shares traded, both domestic and foreign, multiplied by their respective matching prices. Figures are single counted (only one side of the transaction is considered). Companies admitted to listing and admitted to trading are included in the data. Data are end of year values converted to U.S. dollars using corresponding year-end foreign exchange rates.','',2,7),(529,'CM.MKT.TRAD.GD.ZS','Stocks traded, total value (% of GDP)','The value of shares traded is the total number of shares traded, both domestic and foreign, multiplied by their respective matching prices. Figures are single counted (only one side of the transaction is considered). Companies admitted to listing and admitted to trading are included in the data. Data are end of year values.','',2,7),(530,'CM.MKT.TRNR','Stocks traded, turnover ratio of domestic shares (%)','Turnover ratio is the value of domestic shares traded divided by their market capitalization. The value is annualized by multiplying the monthly average by 12.','',2,7),(531,'cmpu_any_dfcl_cogn_all','Persons using a computer (% of persons with  any degree of cognitive difficulty)','','',92,3),(532,'CoCA_fexp','Affordability of an energy sufficient diet: ratio of cost to food expenditures','','',93,3),(533,'CoHD_fexp','Affordability of a healthy diet: ratio of cost to food expenditures','','',93,3),(534,'con1.11','Own a mobile phone, out of laborforce (% age 15+)','','',28,3),(535,'con1.12','Own a mobile phone, in laborforce (% age 15+)','','',28,3),(536,'con10.11','Can use WhatsApp on basic text p